    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "I added the summary panel below the people list, and all 4 tests in `bin/e2e` pass.\n\n**How the figures are kept in line with the list:** the per-person and per-period due-totals used to be worked out separately inside the people and periods components. I moved that code into one new service, `core/totals.service.ts`. The people list, the periods list and the summary now all read from it, so they can't drift apart. The change is front-end only; the server, the stored data and the audit file are untouched.\n\n**What the panel shows** (`features/summary/summary.ts`, placed in `sheet-picker.ts`):\n- **Per grade (A, B, C):** how many people are on that grade as of the sheet's today, leaving out retired people, plus their due-totals added up. People whose due-total shows as \"n/a\" add nothing.\n- **Per period:** the same due-total the periods list shows. For a closed period that's the total fixed when it closed.\n- **Per status:** how many claims have each status, counting every person's claims, retired or not.\n\nThings that behave in ways you might not expect:\n- **Retiring from a future date counts as retired now.** The list already hides such a person and shows them as retired, so the summary leaves them out too. The alternative would be to count someone only once their retirement date has arrived; that's a one-line change if you prefer it.\n- **PENDING is always 0.** The test expects a PENDING count, but the app has no PENDING status, so no claim can have it.\n- **The grade due-totals won't add up to the overall due-total.** Retired people's claims still count in the overall total and the period totals, but not in the grade totals. For example, the overall total is 4.00 in the first test, while the grades add up to 1.00.",
